// titan.ron — the titan kinds. Named after the generic term **Titan**
// (sheet 10_Namensschema: „Gattungsbegriff, bleibt"), no plural s, like the project name.
//
// At least HALF of all kinds carry an anti-autopilot property (bible 4) —
// otherwise combat degenerates into clicking the mouse on targets.
// windup_s is >= 0.4 everywhere (bible, pillar P4). tests/data.rs checks that.
// ⚠️ UNTUNED, see game.ron.
//
// **No height stands in this file.** Every kind names only its `size_class` — one of the
// five classes out of assets/data/scale.ron (F-064, given by the user 2026-08-09).
// Reason: the height and the cortex height belong together. Standing here per kind, they
// would be sixteen numbers nobody checks against each other — and the first one somebody
// forgets to update leaves the cortex sitting in the belly.
// tests/data.rs falls over when a kind names a class that does not exist.
//
// Until 2026-08-09 the field was called `size`. Renamed, because a name ending in "-size"
// sounds like a LENGTH (docs/conventions.md §5: field names carry their unit) — but what
// stands here is a key, not a value in meters.
//
// ⚠️ `cortex_radius_m` is the only value in this file that did NOT follow the size table:
// the bodies have grown by up to a factor of two, the hit zone has not.
// Relative to body height the target halved right across all kinds (bellower:
// 14.0 % -> 6.7 %). That is a balancing crack and not a question of form; it stands as
// docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-019 with an assumption and a place to take it back. Corrected below
// are only the two GEOMETRICALLY impossible cases (cortex larger than the whole head) —
// not the ratio as a whole.
//
// ⚠️ `strike_half_angle_deg` is NEW (2026-08-13, Q-031 option 2) and it is UNTUNED. It stood at
// 55.0 on every kind — a guess nobody had played — until 2026-08-19; see the spread at the
// bottom of this header. It is half the arc a blow can land in,
// off the titan's own forward vector, on the ground plane: with `attack_range_m` it makes the
// strike a CONE. Until it existed the strike was a cylinder and a player in the titan's back
// took exactly what a player in his face took (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-012), which is why
// `turn_deg_per_s` above governed nothing. Range [30, 90], guarded by
// tests/combat.rs::every_kind_carries_a_strike_half_angle_in_range.
// ⚠️ `stagger_s` is NEW (2026-08-19, F-032) and it is the FIRST consequence a non-lethal cut
// has ever had in this game. Measured the same day (scripts/f032-swords.txt): a blade through
// the chest, the arm and the leg all wrote `TitanHit { zone: Torso }` at 20.67 m/s and NOTHING
// in the repository read it — titan::brain::receive_hits drops every non-cortex hit and the
// only reaction left was `gear.ron: feel.hit_stop_normal_s` = 2 ticks = 33 ms, which no player
// can see. This number is what a body cut buys instead: seconds in which the titan's ADVANCE
// stops. It is the backlog's own middle option ("Kein Kill, sondern Stagger, Bewegungs-Debuff
// oder Blendung") and deliberately not the other two — a fall (Beintreffer) and a blinding
// (Augentreffer) are TitanState work in src/titan/, and the per-limb hitboxes they would have
// to be triggered from do not exist yet either (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-109).
//
// ⚠️ HARD UPPER BOUND, not taste: one player's two blades land every
// `(swing_s + cooldown_s) / 2` = 0.325 s (gear.ron). At or above that a titan never gets a
// tick to move in and the stagger is a permanent lock —
// tests/combat.rs::f032_no_kind_can_be_tuned_into_a_permanent_stagger_lock reads both files
// and falls over on it. Every value below sits under 0.27.
// The spread is by mass: the 4.2 m scuttler is thrown off his line, the 21 m bellower shrugs.
// ⚠️ UNTUNED — nobody has played any of these eight numbers.
//
// **That spread was taken on 2026-08-19** (`F-051` roster round) and it is still UNTUNED, but
// it is no longer uniform: the husk keeps 55.0 because every other measured number in this
// repository is calibrated against him (F-030, q030, q031, F-034, F-032) and moving him would
// move the evidence. The others follow the shape of the blow, not a curve:
//   scuttler 40  a leap is a line, not a sweep — and the line is what you dodge upward out of
//   weaver   45  a short body's short arc
//   errant   50  fast and narrow; he catches you by being where you did not lead
//   husk     55  the baseline, and the calibration. DO NOT MOVE.
//   chorus   55  a husk's arc — the pair is the mechanic, not the swing
//   lurker   60  a snatch out of the air; behind him is still safe
//   warden   75  the shielding hand sweeps wide, which is the price of his open flank
//   bellower 85  21 m of arm; there is almost no behind
//
// ⚠️ `behaviour` is NEW (2026-08-19, F-057..F-063) and it is the first thing in this file that
// is not a number: it is what makes a kind a kind. Read `docs/gameplay/enemies.md` before
// touching a row — the identities are designed, not invented here.
(
    kinds: {
        // Basis: learning the approach angle. The standard titan on which the player
        // practices the first slash — hence the medium class (10 m) and not the small one.
        // 5.0 m -> 10.0 m (class `medium`)
        "husk":     (size_class: "medium",       speed_m_s:  3.0, cortex_radius_m: 0.55,
                     regen_per_s: 0.8, windup_s: 0.60, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 50.0, accel_m_s2: 3.0,
                     strike_s: 0.2, recover_s: 0.4,
                     attack_range_m: 6.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 55.0, attack_cooldown_s: 1.5,
                     stagger_s: 0.22,
                     aggro_radius_m: 45.0, death_s: 1.0, health: 100.0, damage: 34.0,
                     model: "titan_husk",
                     // **The teaching piece, and it stays one.** Eight zeros and an open nape:
                     // whatever the player learns on a husk is the approach angle and nothing
                     // else. Every number of the husk's is also the calibration other things
                     // are pinned to (F-030, q030, q031, F-034, F-032) — it is the one row in
                     // this file that is NOT free to be made interesting.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Unpredictable changes of direction -> leading your shots. Stays husk-sized:
        // the surprise lies in the movement, not in the bulk.
        // 5.0 m -> 10.0 m (class `medium`)
        "errant":   (size_class: "medium",       speed_m_s:  6.5, cortex_radius_m: 0.50,
                     regen_per_s: 0.8, windup_s: 0.45, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 100.0, accel_m_s2: 6.0,
                     strike_s: 0.18, recover_s: 0.37,
                     attack_range_m: 6.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 50.0, attack_cooldown_s: 1.2,
                     stagger_s: 0.20,
                     aggro_radius_m: 50.0, death_s: 1.0, health: 100.0, damage: 30.0,
                     model: "titan_husk",
                     // 35 degrees to each side, flipping every 0.9 s. He still closes —
                     // cos 35 deg = 0.82 of 6.5 m/s is 5.3 m/s straight at you — but he is
                     // never on the line you aimed at. That is the whole lesson: lead him.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 35.0, swerve_period_s: 0.9, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Very fast, jump attack -> vertical evasion. **The fast small one.**
        // The smallest class is his role: small enough to fit between houses.
        // 3.5 m -> 4.2 m (class `small`)
        // cortex_radius_m 0.40 -> 0.20: 0.40 m of radius is 0.80 m of diameter, and the
        // WHOLE head of a 4.2 m titan is only 0.42..0.47 m tall under the user's head rule
        // (scale.ron: titan.min_head_fraction/-max_head_fraction). The cortex was thus
        // almost twice the size of the head — geometrically impossible, and in the image
        // the small titan wears a bullseye instead of a neck. The error is older than the
        // size table (3.5 m body, 0.80 m cortex), it has only become measurable now,
        // because the head rule stands as a number in the RON. Upper bound: 0.47/2 = 0.233 m.
        "scuttler": (size_class: "small",        speed_m_s: 11.0, cortex_radius_m: 0.20,
                     regen_per_s: 1.1, windup_s: 0.40, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 150.0, accel_m_s2: 12.0,
                     strike_s: 0.2, recover_s: 0.4,
                     attack_range_m: 2.5, strike_half_angle_deg: 40.0, attack_cooldown_s: 0.9,
                     stagger_s: 0.26,
                     aggro_radius_m: 60.0, death_s: 0.8, health: 60.0, damage: 18.0,
                     model: "titan_husk",
                     // The leap: 14 m/s carried through a 0.2 s `Strike` is 2.8 m of ground
                     // covered while the blow is already in the air, on top of an
                     // `attack_range_m` of 2.5. Sidestepping at range no longer works — the
                     // answer is altitude, which is what the design asks the scuttler to teach.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 14.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Evasive roll with i-frames -> timing instead of spam. A roll needs a short
        // body; 4.5 m sat between `small` (4.2) and `medium` (10) — decided for
        // `small`, because 10 m no longer roll away credibly.
        // 4.5 m -> 4.2 m (class `small`)
        // cortex_radius_m 0.45 -> 0.23: the same arithmetic as for the scuttler, at the
        // upper bound (0.233 m) instead of below it — the weaver is the timing opponent,
        // his window is the i-frame roll, not a smaller target.
        "weaver":   (size_class: "small",        speed_m_s:  7.0, cortex_radius_m: 0.23,
                     regen_per_s: 1.0, windup_s: 0.50, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 120.0, accel_m_s2: 8.0,
                     strike_s: 0.18, recover_s: 0.37,
                     attack_range_m: 2.5, strike_half_angle_deg: 45.0, attack_cooldown_s: 1.1,
                     stagger_s: 0.24,
                     aggro_radius_m: 50.0, death_s: 0.8, health: 70.0, damage: 22.0,
                     model: "titan_husk",
                     // **The nape is shut except inside his own attack.** Not a roll with
                     // i-frames — a roll needs a `TitanState` arm and `shared/state.rs` is not
                     // this hand's this round — but the SAME lesson, and the smaller version of
                     // it: you cannot cut a weaver whenever you like. Bait the wind-up, and the
                     // 0.5 + 0.18 + 0.37 = 1.05 s of commitment is your window.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: WhenCommitted,
                      // **The roll** (F-059), and it is the second half of the same lesson the
                      // guard above is the first half of. He is only cuttable inside his own
                      // attack — and the attack now ENDS in a roll instead of walking back into
                      // Pursue: 0.15 s of crouch with the nape still open (a LONGER window than
                      // he had, and the readable startup the design asks for), then 0.30 s in
                      // which the cortex sensor is out of the world while he travels
                      // 9.0 * 0.45 = 4.05 m backwards.
                      // What it costs the player: a blade thrown at the end of the window
                      // catches nothing, and he has to close 4 m again. That is
                      // "timing instead of spam" with a number on it.
                      // ⚠️ UNTUNED. All three are guesses; roll_startup_s is the one that
                      // decides whether the tell can be read at all.
                      roll_s: 0.45, roll_startup_s: 0.15, roll_speed_m_s: 9.0)),
        // Shields the cortex with his hand -> arms first, then cortex. The cover only works
        // when the hand really does hide the cortex: for that he needs arm length.
        // 8.0 m -> 14.0 m (class `large`)
        "warden":   (size_class: "large", speed_m_s:  3.5, cortex_radius_m: 0.60,
                     regen_per_s: 0.6, windup_s: 0.70, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 40.0, accel_m_s2: 3.0,
                     strike_s: 0.22, recover_s: 0.48,
                     attack_range_m: 8.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 75.0, attack_cooldown_s: 2.0,
                     stagger_s: 0.14,
                     aggro_radius_m: 40.0, death_s: 1.4, health: 220.0, damage: 55.0,
                     model: "titan_large",
                     // **The hand is on the nape until you cut it off the nape.** Any body
                     // cut opens him for 3.0 s — and the same cut already staggers him for
                     // `stagger_s` 0.14 s (F-032), so the two-stage attack is one loop and not
                     // two: graze the torso, he stumbles, the nape is a target for three
                     // seconds. Without the body cut the blade passes through a covered neck
                     // and books a torso hit instead, which opens him — so the fight teaches
                     // itself.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: WhenOpened(3.0),
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Motionless ambush, grab out of the air -> changes of height, attention.
        // "Out of the air" is a question of reach: at 10 m he grabs below the roof, at
        // 14 m above the roof — only then does he force a change of height.
        // 5.0 m -> 14.0 m (class `large`), model titan_husk -> titan_large
        "lurker":   (size_class: "large", speed_m_s:  2.0, cortex_radius_m: 0.50,
                     regen_per_s: 0.7, windup_s: 0.40, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 45.0, accel_m_s2: 2.0,
                     strike_s: 0.2, recover_s: 0.45,
                     attack_range_m: 8.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 60.0, attack_cooldown_s: 1.8,
                     stagger_s: 0.16,
                     aggro_radius_m: 25.0, death_s: 1.4, health: 200.0, damage: 48.0,
                     model: "titan_large",
                     // **He never takes a step.** `ambush` cuts `Idle -> Pursue` out of his
                     // state machine entirely: he stands until you are inside his 8 m reach,
                     // strikes, and stands again. Inside his `aggro_radius_m` of 25 he still
                     // turns on the spot — an ambusher who cannot come about swings his cone at
                     // empty street. A lurker you have seen is free; a lurker you have not seen
                     // costs 48.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: true,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Reacts to the sound of gas, calls in reinforcements -> resource discipline.
        // **The big loud one.** He was the largest kind so far and stays it: the largest
        // class that occurs in a normal mission.
        // 10.0 m -> 21.0 m (class `huge`)
        "bellower": (size_class: "huge",        speed_m_s:  2.5, cortex_radius_m: 0.70,
                     regen_per_s: 0.5, windup_s: 0.80, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 30.0, accel_m_s2: 2.0,
                     strike_s: 0.25, recover_s: 0.55,
                     attack_range_m: 12.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 85.0, attack_cooldown_s: 2.5,
                     stagger_s: 0.10,
                     aggro_radius_m: 70.0, death_s: 1.6, health: 320.0, damage: 70.0,
                     model: "titan_large",
                     // 90 m of call — every idle titan inside it starts walking the moment the
                     // bellower acquires you. ⚠️ **The ear is missing.** The design has him
                     // react to the SOUND OF GAS (F-051, F-062); no perception model exists, so
                     // today he calls on sight, at `aggro_radius_m` 70. The stealth layer is
                     // the ear, not the call, and it is honestly not built.
                     // ⚠️ **And he cannot spawn**: he is class `huge` and
                     // `scale.ron: max_spawnable_class` is `large` (Q-028). See FIND-118.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 0.0, call_radius_m: 90.0, call_hold_s: 25.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),
        // Pairs cover each other -> target prioritization, separation. Two equally large
        // standard titans; the task is the pair, not the single body.
        // 5.0 m -> 10.0 m (class `medium`)
        "chorus":   (size_class: "medium",       speed_m_s:  5.0, cortex_radius_m: 0.50,
                     regen_per_s: 0.9, windup_s: 0.55, 
                     turn_deg_per_s: 55.0, accel_m_s2: 4.0,
                     strike_s: 0.2, recover_s: 0.4,
                     attack_range_m: 6.0, strike_half_angle_deg: 55.0, attack_cooldown_s: 1.4,
                     stagger_s: 0.20,
                     aggro_radius_m: 45.0, death_s: 1.0, health: 100.0, damage: 30.0,
                     model: "titan_husk",
                     // 9 m of flank offset, with the sign taken off the titan's own id — so
                     // chorus #1 aims 9 m to your left and chorus #2 aims 9 m to your right,
                     // and they close on you from two sides instead of stacking in one
                     // silhouette. Turning your back on one is the price of facing the other:
                     // target prioritization, which is what the pair is for.
                     behaviour: (swerve_deg: 0.0, swerve_period_s: 0.0, lunge_m_s: 0.0,
                      flank_offset_m: 9.0, call_radius_m: 0.0, call_hold_s: 0.0, ambush: false,
                      cortex_guard: Always,
                      roll_s: 0.0, roll_startup_s: 0.0, roll_speed_m_s: 0.0)),

        // The class `boss` (28 m) is deliberately unused: its first representative is a
        // raid opponent, and raids stand behind the Vector Gear gate (docs/ROADMAP.md).
    },
)
